==English==
{{wp|do}}

===Etymology 1===
{{wp|do-support}}
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:don<id:to do>}}

From {{inh|en|enm|don}}, from {{inh|en|ang|don|dōn}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*dōn}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*dōną}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dʰeh₁-||to put, place, do, make}}.

For senses 4 and 5, compare {{cog|non|duga}}, also Northern {{cog|en|dow}}.

The past tense form is from {{inh|en|enm|didde}}, {{m|enm|dude}}, from {{inh|en|ang|dyde}}, {{m|ang|*diede}}, an unexpected development from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*dedǭ}}/{{m|gem-pro|*dedē}} (the expected reflex would be ''*ded''), from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*dʰédʰeh₁ti}}, an athematic e-reduplicated verb of the same root {{m|ine-pro|*dʰeh₁-}}.

The meaningless use of ''do'' in interrogative, negative, and affirmative sentences (e.g. "''Do'' you like painting?" "Yes, I ''do''"), existing in some form in most Germanic languages,<ref name=Langer>{{cite-book|last=Langer|first=Nils|title=Linguistic Purism in Action: How auxiliary tun was stigmatized in Early New High German|year=2001|publisher=de Gruyter|isbn=9783110881103|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=IZUiAAAAQBAJ}}</ref> is thought by some linguists to be one of the {{w|Brittonicisms in English}}, calqued from [[Brythonic]].<ref>{{cite-journal|author=John McWhorter|title=What else happened to English? A brief for the Celtic hypothesis|journal=English language & linguistics|location=Cambridge|publisher=University Press|year=2009|volume=13|issue=2|pages=163-191}}</ref> It is first recorded in Middle English, where it may have marked the [[perfective]] aspect, though in some cases the meaning seems to be [[imperfective]]. In {{w|Early Modern English}}, any meaning in such contexts was lost, making it a dummy auxiliary, and soon thereafter its use became mandatory in most questions and negations.

Doublets include {{m|en|deed}}, {{m|en|deem}}, and {{m|en|-dom}}, but not {{m|en|deal}}.

Other cognates include, via Latin, {{cog|en|feast}}, {{m|en|festival}}, {{m|en|fair||celebration}}, via Greek, {{cog|en|theo-}}, {{m|en|theme}}, {{m|en|thesis}}, and {{cog|sa|दधाति||to put}}, {{m|sa|धातृ||creator}} and {{m|sa|धातु||layer, element, root}}.

